evidence at the macro level. Crucially, though, their tests were conducted within a linear framework: they checked whether the linear slope differed across subgroups, hot versus cold countries, rich versus poor, but never asked whether the relationship itself was curved. For rich countries clustered near the peak of what would turn out to be an inverted-U, the positive effects …</p><p>URL: <a href="https://carloschavezp29.substack.com/p/why-economists-linearize-everything?utm_source=substack&utm_campaign=post_embed&utm_medium=web">https://carloschavezp29.substack.com/p/why-economists-linearize-everything?utm_source=substack&utm_campaign=post_embed&utm_medium=web</a></p><p>source: <a href="https://arnoldkling.substack.com/p/economics-links-2162026">Economics Links, 2/16/2026</a></p>]]></description><link>https://carloschavezp29.substack.com/p/why-economists-linearize-everything?utm_source=substack&amp;utm_campaign=post_embed&amp;utm_medium=web</link><guid isPermaLink="true">https://carloschavezp29.substack.com/p/why-economists-linearize-everything?utm_source=substack&amp;utm_campaign=post_embed&amp;utm_medium=web</guid><pubDate>Mon, 16 Feb 2026 13:03:23 GMT</pubDate></item></channel></rss>